{
  "id": "bigville_clerk_treasurer",
  "version": "1.0.0",
  "depends_on": [],
  "self_load_only": true,
  "description": "bigville R46 (block 345000): the CLERK-TREASURER -- a PAID council office whose job is to KEEP RECORDS and COLLECT TAXES. Money is not magic: the treasury/bank only gains coin because someone COLLECTS it (ct_collect; no clerk in office -> the treasury stays flat), and every move is WRITTEN into a Ledger (recorded_balance + a LedgerEntry per transaction -- someone has to do the books). The clerk is PAID a salary from the treasury, itself a RECORDED expense (ct_pay_clerk). RECONCILIATION (ct_reconcile) checks the written record against the actual coin: an honest, diligent clerk reconciles to 0; an UNRECORDED transaction (actual > recorded) or EMBEZZLEMENT (the clerk records the full tax but banks only honesty*tax, so recorded > actual) surfaces as a non-zero discrepancy -- the books do not balance themselves. Fiscal sibling of the Constable (339000): a public function is an office-holder's JOB, not an automatic effect.",
  "nodes": [],
  "edges": [],
  "productions": [],
  "concepts": [],
  "rules": [
    {
      "name": "ct_collect",
      "batch": false,
      "comment": "THE CLERK-TREASURER collects a resident's tax AND writes the record -- money does not magically appear: with no clerk in office this rule never fires and the treasury stays flat. The resident pays the full assessed tax; only honesty*tax reaches the treasury (a dishonest clerk pockets the rest); the clerk RECORDS the full amount (ledger.recorded += tax_due) and files a LedgerEntry. So a dishonest clerk makes recorded > actual -- the gap the reconciliation will catch.",
      "match": [
        [
          "town",
          "has_clerk",
          "clerk"
        ],
        [
          "town",
          "has_treasury",
          "treasury"
